            <ArticleTitle>Vitamin D deficiency &amp; childhood obesity: a tale of two epidemics.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>49-53</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obesity and vitamin D deficiency represent two of the most wide-spread health concerns in the United States, especially among children. There is a well-established inverse relationship between vitamin D status and obesity; however, it is unknown as to whether vitamin D deficiency contributes to, or is a consequence of obesity. Based on available research, the positive effects of correcting hypovitaminosis D in obesity seem to be primarily related to its action on glycemic control.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
